Combat in ''Human Host'' spans '''melee''', '''ranged''', '''throwable''', and '''vehicle''' engagement, with stamina and durability as the two universal constraints.

== Damage pipeline ==

All damage in the game, from zombies, environmental hazards, and explosions, funnels through a single entry point (<code>Battle_Info.MinusHP</code>). This has two practical consequences:

* '''Armor works uniformly.''' A helmet reduces zombie-bite damage the same way it reduces vehicle-collision damage.
* '''Damage mods stack predictably.''' Perk factors that reduce incoming damage apply to every source.

== Melee ==

Melee weapons split into '''blades''' (higher damage) and '''blunt''' (higher stagger). See [[Melee Weapons]].

* '''Stamina.''' Each swing drains stamina. Out of stamina means swings do minimal damage.
* '''Durability.''' Every hit deducts durability. Repair at the matching workbench.
* '''Blocking.''' Right mouse raises a weapon or shield; blocks reduce damage proportional to weapon block value.

== Ranged ==

Ranged weapons include bows, crossbows, and firearms. See [[Ranged Weapons]] and [[Ammunition]].

* '''Ammo scarcity''' is the primary balance lever early game. Hoard for horde nights.
* '''Fire rate''' vs '''magazine size''' is the second tradeoff.
* '''Aim sway''' increases at low stamina.

== Throwables ==

Grenades and other throwables deal area damage and are consumed on throw.

== Blocking and staggering ==

Weapon block values are printed in each weapon's [[Template:Infobox weapon|infobox]]. Blocking timed near an incoming hit reduces damage and staggers the attacker. Staggered zombies take a brief bonus-damage window.

== Horde nights ==

Every in-game night triggers a horde spawn. Horde size scales with:

* '''Player level'''
* '''Base value''' (larger, more elaborate bases attract more zombies)
* '''Biome''' ([[Warzone]] and [[Wasteland]] have permanently elevated horde densities)

Base defense tips:

* '''Traps.''' Spike variants, sensor spikes, rotating blades, and laser trip-triggers chew through hordes efficiently. See [[Building#Traps]] for the full catalog.
* '''Chokepoints.''' Force a single-file approach; one melee player can hold a doorway.
* '''Elevation.''' Zombies are poor climbers.

